    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • XPENG Reveals G9L at the 2026 Paris Motor Show, Dubbed As the Ultimate \"Large SUV for the AI Era\"\nCertain 2026 Ram 2500 Trucks Could Lose Stability Control Without Warning\n32 photos\nPhoto: Ram\nTowing 20,000 pounds, which is 9,072 kilos in metric speak, in a properly equipped 2026 Ram 2500 is asking a lot of the cooling system, driveline, and brakes. You are further asking a lot of the ESC, specifically the system that decides which wheel to brake and how much torque to cut when the back end starts to dance.\nFCA US LLC has recalled 8,329 of these pickups because a steering column module can switch electronic stability control off without giving the driver any warning. Referred to as 26V651 in NHTSA vernacular, the campaign covers 2026 models exclusively. Affected trucks were produced between September 13, 2025, and November 15, 2025, for the United States market.\nThe subject module lives in the steering column and handles a bunch of driver inputs and related signals. If the SCCM develops an internal fault, said module can disable electronic stability control, thus increasing the risk of a crash at high speeds. Per Federal Motor Vehicle Safety Standard 126, electronic stability control must stay active while driving.\nAs for the part that should bother affected owners, the ESC light only comes on after the module resets. A three-quarter-ton pickup is not the kind of vehicle where you want to experience that while towing on the highway. The 2026 model offers a 6.4-liter HEMI naturally aspirated V8 with quite a bit of torque under its belt, or an optional 6.7-liter Cummins turbo diesel I6 with quite a bit more torque.\nThe advertised maximum towing capacity of 20,000 pounds is for properly equipped Cummins trucks. Electronic stability control uses targeted braking and engine torque management to keep the vehicle on the driver's intended line. Losing ESC when intervention is relied upon is the perfect recipe for disaster.\nPhoto: Ram Stellantis North America started investigating the recall condition back in July 2026. Field records and customer assistance records were reviewed through September 2026, helping the Technical Safety and Regulatory Compliance group recognize the noncompliance condition. By the end of September 2026, the Vehicle Regulations Committee had heard enough.\nRather than a reflash of the module, the remedy comes in the guise of a replacement module. A newly designed steering column module will be fitted at no charge to affected customers. Ram dealers nationwide have already been notified, while customer notifications are due on November 24, 2026, at the earliest. VINs for suspect trucks are now searchable on the NHTSA's website.\nCompeting against Ford 's F-250, the Chevrolet division's Silverado 2500, and the mechanically similar GMC Sierra 2500, the Ram 2500 carries a starting price of $46,855 for model year 2026. Its base specification is the Tradesman grade with rear-wheel drive, the regular cab, eight-foot box, and the HEMI engine.\nFor 2027, the starting price is $48,040 before destination charge. Beyond the work-oriented trim, prospective customers are further presented with the Big Horn, Black Express, and Laramie trims. Those prepared to spend in excess of $70,000 on a 2500 are presented with the Rebel, Power Wagon, Limited, and Longhorn. The latter starts at $75,505 sans the $2,795 freight charge.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nram hd Ram 2500 Ram recall US\nDownload: Ram 2500 ESC recall (PDF)\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT Tiny Hot Hatch Just Sold for Porsche Money, Has a Punchy V6 Behind the Seats and a Fatty Widebody",
